## 5. Naming Standards

This section covers Global Config Naming, APIKit Flow Naming, Flow & Sub-flow Naming, and Flow & Component Design standards.

### 5.1 Global Config Naming

#### All config names must end with `_config`

- **Rule:** Every `name` attribute on connector configs, listener configs, APIKit config, and similar elements must end with `_config`.
- **Format:** `{description}_config` (use hyphens within the description part).
- **Examples:**
  - `api-listener_config` (HTTP listener)
  - `sf-http-sys-api-template_http-listener_config` (not `_httpListenerConfig` — no camelCase)
  - `sf-http-sys-api-template_config` (not `-config`)
  - `custom-logger-connector_config` (not `_connectorConfig` — use hyphens, not underscores)
  - `http-token-server_http-request_config` (HTTP request config)
  - `azure-key-vault-properties-provider_config`

> This ruleset supersedes the older `{description}_{type}` camelCase form (e.g., `customer-sapi_httpListenerConfig`) defined in `review-mule-global-config.mdc`.

#### HTTP listener config naming

- **Rule:** No camelCase; use hyphens to separate words; end with `_config`.
- **Example:** `api-listener_config`, `sf-http-sys-api-template_http-listener_config`

#### Apply to

- `http:listener-config`
- `apikit:config`
- `module-logger:config`
- `http:request-config`
- `objectstore:object-store`
- `configuration-properties` doc:name (e.g., `common-properties_config`, `env-properties_config`)
- `api-gateway:autodiscovery` doc:name (e.g., `api-autodiscovery_config`)
- Any other connector or config element with a `name` attribute

### 5.2 APIKit Flow Naming

#### Flow names include config name

- **Rule:** APIKit-generated flow names use the pattern `{method}:{path}:{configName}`.
- When the config name changes (e.g., to end with `_config`), all flow names and `flow-ref` references must be updated accordingly.
- **Example:** `get:\ping:sf-http-sys-api-template-spec_config`, `post:\records:application\json:sf-http-sys-api-template-spec_config`

#### Path parameters use parentheses in flow names

- **Rule:** In Mule API spec flow names, path parameters use `()` (parentheses), not `{}` (curly braces).
- RAML defines paths like `/patients/leads/{id}`; the corresponding Mule flow name uses `\(id)` in the path segment.
- **Example:** `put:\records\(id):application\json:sf-http-sys-api-template-spec_config` (not `\{id\}`).
- **uriParams:** `attributes.uriParams` uses the RAML parameter name (e.g. `attributes.uriParams.id` for path param `{id}`).

#### Do not rename APIKit-generated flows

- **Do not rename** flows generated by APIKit scaffolding. They follow `{method}:\{resource}:{configName}` (e.g., `get:\ping:sf-http-sys-api-template-spec_config`).
- Replace hardcoded example response transforms with flow references to implementation sub-flows.

### 5.3 Flow & Sub-flow Naming

#### Suffixes

- **Flows:** Suffixed with `-flow` (e.g. `{downstream-sys-api}-request-flow`, `sf-http-sys-api-template-main`).
- **Sub-flows:** Suffixed with `-sub-flow` (e.g. `connectivity-error-handler-sub-flow`, `create-record-sub-flow`).
- **Main flow:** `{api-spec}-main` (e.g. `sf-http-sys-api-template-main`).

#### Variables

- **Variables:** `camelCase` (e.g., `requestContext`, `httpStatus`, `targetApiParams`).

### 5.4 Flow & Component Design

- Every main flow must begin with **context-variable setup** (per pattern from §3): `requestContext` (HTTP listener APIs) or `initialVar` (event-driven flows), followed by a **Start Log**, and end with an **End Log** (using `module-logger`).
- All loggers use `sourceSystem`/`targetSystem` from the chosen context variable (`vars.requestContext.*` or `vars.initialVar.*`).
- Limit flow complexity: if a flow exceeds ~10 components, break logic into meaningful sub-flows.
- **Message processors:** Never leave default Anypoint Studio names. Use descriptive names (e.g., `Set HTTP Status Code`, `Transform Composite Response`, `LOG BEFORE REQUEST`).
- **`doc:name` AND `doc:id` are required on every element** — flow, sub-flow, and every processor/scope. `doc:id` must be a unique UUID-format string (e.g., `a1b2c3d4-0001-0001-0001-000000000001`). Missing either breaks Anypoint Studio's visual flow mapping. Example:

```xml
<ee:transform doc:name="FV Set Request Context" doc:id="a1b2c3d4-0001-0001-0001-000000000001">
    <ee:message />
    <ee:variables>
        <ee:set-variable resource="dwls/v-request-context.dwl" variableName="requestContext" />
    </ee:variables>
</ee:transform>
```

### 5.5 Configuration Element Placement

- All global configuration elements must be defined in `src/main/mule/common/global-config.xml`.
- No config elements in implementation flow files.

